LEGAL NOTICE
(Cease & Desist — Under the Trade Marks Act, 1999)

To,
{respondent}
{respondentAddress}

From,
{applicant}
Through: {advocateName}, Advocate
{advocateAddress}

prayerTemplate:
You are hereby called upon to, within {response_days} ({response_days_words}) days of receipt of this notice:
(a) IMMEDIATELY CEASE AND DESIST from all use, in any manner whatsoever — including manufacturing, marketing, advertising, selling, offering for sale, importing, exporting, or displaying on any medium including online platforms — of the mark '{infringing_mark}' or any mark deceptively similar to my client's registered trademark '{client_mark}' (Registration No. {registration_number}, Class {class_nice});
(b) Recall from the market and DELIVER UP to my client all infringing goods, packaging, labels, brochures, signage, and marketing material bearing the impugned mark, for destruction;
(c) DESTROY all moulds, plates, digital files, and means of production of the impugned mark;
(d) Remove and de-list all online listings (including on e-commerce marketplaces, social media, and your own websites) bearing the impugned mark, and provide proof thereof;
(e) Render a true and faithful account of profits earned from sale of infringing goods/services, OR pay damages in the sum of Rs. {damages_amount}/- at my client's election;
(f) Furnish a written UNDERTAKING, supported by an affidavit, that you shall not, directly or indirectly, by yourself or through any agent or assign, use the said impugned mark or any deceptively similar mark in future;
(g) Reimburse the cost of this notice and legal expenses.

Failing the above, my client shall be constrained to file a suit for permanent injunction, delivery up, damages, account of profits, and costs, before the competent court of jurisdiction under Section 134 of the Trade Marks Act, 1999 — and seek interim and ad-interim injunctions including ex-parte orders — entirely at your risk, cost, and consequences.

This notice is without prejudice to and reserves all rights of my client, including the right to initiate criminal proceedings under Sections 103, 104 and 105 of the Trade Marks Act, 1999.
